{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1545 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "16b565a3ca0e7316a5414f16fc6b77e4014bc9e11bce7737dcb971c1beba0700" :edb/db-before-t 1544 :edb/db-after-t 1545 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534857 50 #inst "2026-10-08T09:16:17.264Z" 13194139534857 true] [17592186124202 1100 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+2y^2-2}{x^2}$ are given by $f(x,y) = c$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $c=1$ gives a circle\n2. $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n3. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" 13194139534857 false] [17592186124202 1100 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+2y^2-2}{x^2}$ are given by $f(x,y) = c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=1$ gives a circle\n\n- $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n\n- $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" 13194139534857 true] [17592186124202 1145 "Setting\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = c\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2} + 2y^{2} - 2}{x^{2}} &= c \\\\\nx^{2} + 2y^{2} - 2 &= cx^{2} \\\\\n(1 - c)x^{2} + 2y^{2} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c = 1$, then the level curve is $y^2 = 1$, which consists of two lines.\n- If $c = 0$, then the level curve is $x^2+2y^2=2$, which is an ellipse.\n- If $c = 2$, then the level curve is $2y^2-x^2=2$ which is a hyperbola.\n\nSo, only statements II and III are true." 13194139534857 false] [17592186124202 1145 "Setting $f(x,y) = c$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2}{{x}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2 & =c{x}^{2} \\\\ (1-c){x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c = 1,$ then the level curve is $y^2 = 1,$ which consists of two lines.\n\n- If $c = 0,$ then the level curve is $x^2+2y^2=2,$ which is an ellipse.\n\n- If $c = 2,$ then the level curve is $2y^2-x^2=2$ which is a hyperbola.\n\nSo, only statements II and III are true." 13194139534857 true] [17592186124203 1100 "Sketch the level curves of the surface $z=e^{x^2-y^2}$." 13194139534857 false] [17592186124203 1100 "Sketch the level curves of the surface $z=e^{x^2-y^2}.$" 13194139534857 true] [17592186124203 1145 "The level curves for the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=e^{x^2-y^2}\n$$\n\ntake the form\n\n$$\ne^{x^2-y^2} = c\n$$\n\nNote that we must have the constraint $c\\gt 0$ since $e^x \\gt 0$ for all $x$.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ne^{x^{2} - y^{2}} = c; \\ln (e^{x^{2} - y^{2}}) = \\ln c; x^{2} - y^{2} = \\ln c\n$$\n\nWe can split this into three cases, depending on the sign of $\\ln c$.\n\n- Case 1: $\\ln c \\gt 0$. In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $x^2-y^2 = \\ln c$.\n- Case 2: $\\ln c = 0$. In this case, we have the lines $y=\\pm x$.\n- Case 3: $\\ln c < 0$. In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $y^2 - x^2 = -\\ln c$.\n\nTherefore, the level curves are as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1900/Source/Images/d07a616a086987c45b478dda907c99c1.png)" 13194139534857 false] [17592186124203 1145 "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)=e^{x^2-y^2}$ take the form\n\n$$\ne^{x^2-y^2} = c.\n$$\n\nNote that we must have the constraint $c\\gt 0$ since $e^x \\gt 0$ for all $x.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡c \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can split this into three cases, depending on the sign of $\\ln c.$\n\n- Case 1: $\\ln c \\gt 0.$ In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $x^2-y^2 = \\ln c.$\n\n- Case 2: $\\ln c = 0.$ In this case, we have the lines $y=\\pm x.$\n\n- Case 3: $\\ln c < 0.$ In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $y^2 - x^2 = -\\ln c.$\n\nTherefore, the level curves are as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/5f2ec43639cb31e03cc4a00e43969c7f0333c098fc4b366ea1609772faabfda5.png)" 13194139534857 true] [17592186124280 1100 "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ for the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{12y}{x^2-y^2}$." 13194139534857 false] [17592186124280 1100 "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ for the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{12y}{x^2-y^2}.$" 13194139534857 true] [17592186124280 1145 "First, we find the value of the function at $G$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)&= \\dfrac{12(1)}{\\big(\\sqrt{5}\\big)^2 - 1^2} = 3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = c\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3$. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y) &= c \\\\\n\\frac{12y}{x^{2} - y^{2}} &= 3 \\\\\n4y &= x^{2} - y^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - y^{2} - 4y - 4 &= -4 \\\\\nx^{2} - (y^{2} + 4y + 4) &= -4 \\\\\nx^{2} - (y + 2)^{2} &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the hyperbola\n\n$$\nx^2 - (y+2)^2 = -4\n$$" 13194139534857 false] [17592186124280 1145 "First, we find the value of the function at $G$:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n  f\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)&= \\dfrac{12(1)}{\\big(\\sqrt{5}\\big)^2 - 1^2} = 3}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{12y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ 4y & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4y-4 & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-({y}^{2}+4y+4) & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-(y+2)^{2} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the hyperbola $x^2 - (y+2)^2 = -4.$" 13194139534857 true] [17592186124393 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315322 13194139534857 true] [17592186124393 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315329 13194139534857 true] [17592186124393 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315336 13194139534857 true] [17592186124393 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315385 13194139534857 true] [17592186124393 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315392 13194139534857 true] [17592186124394 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315343 13194139534857 true] [17592186124394 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315350 13194139534857 true] [17592186124394 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315364 13194139534857 true] [17592186124394 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315399 13194139534857 true] [17592186124474 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315308 13194139534857 true] [17592186124474 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315315 13194139534857 true] [17592186124474 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315357 13194139534857 true] [17592186124474 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315371 13194139534857 true] [17592186124474 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315378 13194139534857 true] [17592186125095 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534857 true] [17592186125095 1100 "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x, y) = \\ln (y - 2x^{2})$?" 13194139534857 false] [17592186125095 1100 "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2{x}^{2})?$" 13194139534857 true] [17592186125095 1145 "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nNotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2{x}^{2}) & =c \\\\ y-2{x}^{2} & ={e}^{c} \\\\ y & =2{x}^{2}+{e}^{c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of parabolas with the vertex at $(0,{e}^{c}).$ Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/6521da99a82e38a68a5aa87a0c7c88eacf4ed7b78351af594ec309fa2cb7f57b.png)" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315308 1017 #edb/ref 17592186315309 13194139534857 true] [17592186315308 1089 "q-128447" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315308 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534857 true] [17592186315308 1100 "Determine the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(1,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=xy-2.$" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315308 1113 #uuid "e0e4e5e1-7e08-5442-8bc9-e119961a69a9" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315308 1145 "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =1(1)-2 \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(1,1)$ is the $-1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ xy-2 & =-1 \\\\ xy & =1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(1,1)$ is the hyperbola $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315310 13194139534857 true] [17592186315309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315311 13194139534857 true] [17592186315309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315312 13194139534857 true] [17592186315309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315313 13194139534857 true] [17592186315309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315314 13194139534857 true] [17592186315309 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534857 true] [17592186315309 1041 "selection" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315309 1104 #edb/ref 17592186315311 13194139534857 true] [17592186315309 1110 #uuid "2a12d53b-9d82-5a92-9335-3e2199cf3707" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315310 1034 "y=\\frac{1}{x}-2" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315310 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534857 true] [17592186315310 1111 #uuid "f405929c-6fdb-5231-85fd-d6213baca970" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315311 1034 "y=\\frac{1}{x}" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315311 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534857 true] [17592186315311 1111 #uuid "4c4ef1bd-a644-58af-989d-5fdafe5dd3ba" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315312 1034 "y=\\frac{2}{x}" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315312 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534857 true] [17592186315312 1111 #uuid "7c060524-8b4c-5f9a-9f2a-04b15c1b7957" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315313 1034 "y=x-2" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315313 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534857 true] [17592186315313 1111 #uuid "c07fd626-b5a6-5653-93ad-0822aa402e0c" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315314 1034 "y=2x+1" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315314 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534857 true] [17592186315314 1111 #uuid "a08d6121-a6a1-5e79-ad99-0c8be10fefa5" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315315 1017 #edb/ref 17592186315316 13194139534857 true] [17592186315315 1089 "q-128451" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315315 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534857 true] [17592186315315 1100 "Determine the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(2,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x-{y}^{2}+3}.$" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315315 1113 #uuid "e612596d-991d-5ec0-9e3d-c489ec6c2861" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315315 1145 "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,0) & =\\frac{1}{2-{0}^{2}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-0+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(2,0)$ is the $\\frac{1}{5}$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\frac{1}{5}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-{y}^{2}+3} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 5 & =x-{y}^{2}+3 \\\\ x & ={y}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(2,0)$ is the parabola $x={y}^{2}+2.$" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315316 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315317 13194139534857 true] [17592186315316 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315318 13194139534857 true] [17592186315316 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315319 13194139534857 true] [17592186315316 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315320 13194139534857 true] [17592186315316 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315321 13194139534857 true] [17592186315316 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534857 true] [17592186315316 1041 "selection" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315316 1104 #edb/ref 17592186315319 13194139534857 true] [17592186315316 1110 #uuid "b24702e2-af21-5fbf-922e-dc8036e0e787" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315317 1034 "x=3{y}^{2}+1" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315317 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534857 true] [17592186315317 1111 #uuid "835ba3e7-7076-51e9-87bd-73720884c35e" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315318 1034 "x={y}^{2}+5" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315318 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534857 true] [17592186315318 1111 #uuid "44db6d8c-515a-5b7e-be7c-cd2ca2e5de05" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315319 1034 "x={y}^{2}+2" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315319 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534857 true] [17592186315319 1111 #uuid "7f212c3c-4d87-534a-8095-1d53900b0730" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315320 1034 "x=3-{y}^{2}" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315320 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534857 true] [17592186315320 1111 #uuid "33881671-7340-5a7a-8e00-837ddbea6379" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315321 1034 "x=1-2{y}^{2}" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315321 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534857 true] [17592186315321 1111 #uuid "63ec9d42-18c1-5718-9ea8-318b7e2bce4e" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315322 1017 #edb/ref 17592186315323 13194139534857 true] [17592186315322 1089 "q-129058" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315322 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534857 true] [17592186315322 1100 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+{y}^{2}}{x}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=-1$ gives a line\n- $c=0$ gives a parabola, excluding the origin\n- $c=1$ gives the $x$-axis, excluding the origin" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315322 1113 #uuid "56b2dc5a-8a55-52b8-86dd-0056a08fe2fa" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315322 1145 "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+{y}^{2}}{x} & =c \\\\ x+{y}^{2} & =cx \\\\ x(1-c)+{y}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1,$ the level curve is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2},$ which is a parabola that opens to the left (excluding the origin). So, I is false.\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curves is $x=-{y}^{2},$ which is a parabola that opens to the left (excluding the origin). So, II is true.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis (excluding the origin). So, III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139534857 true] [17592186315323 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315324 13194139534857 true] [17592186315323 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315325 13194139534857 true] [17592186315323 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315326 13194139534857 true] [17592186315323 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315327 13194139534857 true] [17592186315323 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315328 13194139534857 true] [17592186315323 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534857 true] [17592186315323 1041 "selection" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315323 1104 #edb/ref 17592186315324 13194139534857 true] [17592186315323 1110 #uuid "8088bee8-b3a8-5533-b92d-aa613e6a4a89" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315324 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315324 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534857 true] [17592186315324 1111 #uuid "e4e4d524-165c-5206-b7e8-72fead25c64b" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315325 1034 "I only" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315325 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534857 true] [17592186315325 1111 #uuid "8e97890f-697a-510b-99ac-d22dc40f883b" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315326 1034 "III only" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315326 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534857 true] [17592186315326 1111 #uuid "841e4a70-803f-5f58-b9a3-667f0ffdffc8" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315327 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315327 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534857 true] [17592186315327 1111 #uuid "aad28799-dcb3-53ea-9f39-f2520336bd8f" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315328 1034 "II only" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315328 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534857 true] [17592186315328 1111 #uuid "ba8018a8-f1f3-511e-b66a-29289a09a885" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315329 1017 #edb/ref 17592186315330 13194139534857 true] [17592186315329 1089 "q-129062" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315329 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534857 true] [17592186315329 1100 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=0$ gives a parabola\n- $c=1$ gives an ellipse\n- $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315329 1113 #uuid "0b2cfdd5-7257-5027-8ef3-4d8828269d78" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315329 1145 "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {y}^{2} & =c({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ c{x}^{2}+(c-1){y}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is the line $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis. So, I is false.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is the line $x=0,$ which is the $y$-axis. So, II is false.\n\n- If $c=2,$  then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=2\\,⟹\\,2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, the only real solution to this equation is  $(x,y)=(0,0),$ which is not in the domain of $f(x,y)$ because it would result in division by zero. Thus, III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the listed\"." 13194139534857 true] [17592186315330 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315331 13194139534857 true] [17592186315330 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315332 13194139534857 true] [17592186315330 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315333 13194139534857 true] [17592186315330 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315334 13194139534857 true] [17592186315330 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315335 13194139534857 true] [17592186315330 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534857 true] [17592186315330 1041 "selection" 13194139534857 true] [17592186315330 1104 #edb/ref 17592186315332